selector_to_html = {"a[href=\"#exams\"]":"<h2 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Exams<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#exams\" title=\"Link to this heading\">#</a></h2>","a[href=\"lecture-notes/unit-1-introduction-to-measures/02-Cardinality-countability-and-non-negative-sums.html\"]":"<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">2. Cardinality, countability, and non-negative sums<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#cardinality-countability-and-non-negative-sums\" title=\"Link to this heading\">#</a></h1><p>Measure theory repeatedly combines countably many sets and nonnegative\nquantities. Cardinality tells us which collections can be arranged in a\nsequence, while extended nonnegative sums let us add a family without first\nchoosing an ordering. Together, these ideas supply the mechanism behind\ncountable additivity and the model convergence theorems used later for\nintegrals. While arbitrary nonnegative sums are not strictly necessary for developing\nLebesgue integration theory, they are a very useful test case for dealing with\n“generalized sums” beyond the finite algebraic setting.</p><p>After studying these notes, you will be able to do the following.\n</p>","a[href=\"#key-university-dates\"]":"<h2 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Key university dates<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#key-university-dates\" title=\"Link to this heading\">#</a></h2><p>For official University dates, see the <a class=\"reference external\" href=\"https://registrar.uark.edu/academic-dates/academic-semester-calendar/\"><span>Fall 2026</span> academic calendar</a> and the <a class=\"reference external\" href=\"https://registrar.uark.edu/registration/final-exam-schedule/fall-2026-final-exam-schedule.php\">Registrar’s final-exam schedule</a>.</p>","a[href=\"lecture-notes/unit-2-sigma-algebras-and-measures/10-12-sigma-algebras-measures-and-their-properties.html\"]":"<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">4. <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight math-fallback\" data-math-prerendered=\"\" data-tex=\"\\sigma\" data-math-display=\"inline\" data-math-speech=\"sigma\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"Math: sigma. Original TeX: \\sigma\"><mjx-container class=\"MathJax math-svg\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.292ex\" height=\"1ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -431 571 442\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-NCM-I-1D70E\" d=\"M525 431L300 431C252 431 207 416 166 385C102 336 38 250 38 148C38 58 100-11 190-11C257-11 317 18 368 76C417 132 441 194 441 263C441 304 432 338 414 366L515 366C550 366 567 378 567 403C567 426 550 431 525 431M107 121C107 180 121 234 150 282C184 338 228 366 283 366C341 366 370 336 370 275C370 250 365 221 355 187C335 116 302 66 256 39C233 25 211 18 191 18C136 18 107 63 107 121Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D70E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-NCM-I-1D70E\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container></span>-algebras, measures, and their properties<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#sigma-algebras-measures-and-their-properties\" title=\"Link to this heading\">#</a></h1><p>The preceding discussion of outer measure provides a size for every subset of <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight math-fallback\" data-math-prerendered=\"\" data-tex=\"X\" data-math-display=\"inline\" data-math-speech=\"X\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"Math: X. Original TeX: X\"><mjx-container class=\"MathJax math-svg\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: 0;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.925ex\" height=\"1.545ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -683 851 683\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-2-NCM-I-1D44B\" d=\"M834 683C817 683 758 680 741 680C721 680 651 683 631 683C616 683 608 675 608 659C608 650 613 645 624 644C648 641 660 633 660 618C660 609 654 598 642 585L486 417C457 485 429 552 400 620C401 622 402 624 405 626C414 636 431 642 455 644C471 646 479 654 479 667C479 678 473 683 460 683C436 683 359 680 335 680C314 680 245 683 224 683C209 683 202 675 202 659C202 649 211 644 229 644C274 644 288 644 300 615L416 342L208 119C205 116 201 112 196 109C155 66 108 42 53 39C36 38 27 29 27 14C30 5 31 0 44 0C61 0 120 3 137 3C158 3 227 0 248 0C263 0 270 8 270 24C270 33 265 38 254 39C230 41 218 50 218 65C218 75 227 89 244 107C306 173 369 239 430 306C464 225 499 145 532 63C531 61 530 59 527 56C518 47 502 42 478 39C463 37 455 29 455 16C455 5 461 0 474 0L599 3C620 3 690 0 708 0C723 0 731 8 731 23C731 33 724 38 709 39C678 39 658 40 651 44C644 48 636 60 628 80L501 383C569 455 620 509 653 546C677 571 693 588 702 595C739 626 780 642 825 644C846 645 851 650 851 669C848 678 847 683 834 683Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D44B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-2-NCM-I-1D44B\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container></span>, but the Vitali counterexample shows that no countably additive, translation-invariant extension of interval length can be defined on every subset of <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight math-fallback\" data-math-prerendered=\"\" data-tex=\"\\R\" data-math-display=\"inline\" data-math-speech=\"backslash cap R\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"Math: backslash cap R. Original TeX: \\R\"><mjx-container class=\"MathJax math-svg\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.796ex\" height=\"2.262ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 1236 1000\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-3-NCM-N-5C\" d=\"M420-250C436-250 444-242 444-226C444-223 444-220 443-218L101 734C97 745 90 750 79 750C63 750 55 742 55 726C55 723 55 720 56 718L398-234C402-245 409-250 420-250Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-3-NCM-N-52\" d=\"M611 501C611 558 580 604 519 639C468 668 411 683 349 683L35 683L35 644L63 644C98 644 119 641 126 636C133 631 137 620 137 602L137 81C137 63 133 51 126 46C119 41 98 38 63 38L35 38L35-1C62 2 111 3 183 3C256 3 305 2 332-1L332 38L304 38C269 38 248 41 241 46C234 51 230 63 230 81L230 327L340 327C404 327 451 289 462 235C464 224 465 200 465 165C465 138 466 118 467 106C473 22 546-22 634-22C672-22 699-6 714 25C726 48 732 70 732 91C732 104 726 111 715 111C706 111 700 105 699 92C694 36 673 8 638 8C603 8 591 41 585 70C579 104 575 130 572 147L559 226C544 278 507 316 448 339C524 361 611 415 611 501M470 600C491 581 502 548 502 501C502 463 492 431 473 404C450 373 405 357 336 357L230 357L230 609C230 630 236 641 248 642C255 643 274 644 306 644C385 644 426 641 470 600Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mtext\" fill=\"red\" stroke=\"red\"><use data-c=\"5C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-3-NCM-N-5C\"></use><use data-c=\"52\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-3-NCM-N-52\" transform=\"translate(500,0)\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container></span>.\nTo obtain additivity, we must restrict the domain of a measure from <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight math-fallback\" data-math-prerendered=\"\" data-tex=\"2^{X}\" data-math-display=\"inline\" data-math-speech=\"2 to the X power\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"Math: 2 to the X power. Original TeX: 2^{X}\"><mjx-container class=\"MathJax math-svg\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: 0;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.68ex\" height=\"1.914ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -846 1184.7 846\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-4-NCM-N-32\" d=\"M237 666C186 666 143 648 106 612C69 576 50 534 50 483C50 449 75 424 106 424C136 424 161 450 161 480C161 513 137 536 105 536C102 536 100 536 98 535C117 584 161 627 224 627C306 627 352 556 352 470C352 403 318 331 250 255L62 43C49 28 50 29 50 0L421 0L450 180L417 180C409 129 402 100 396 91C391 86 361 84 306 84L139 84L236 179C304 243 390 312 419 365C439 400 449 435 449 470C449 588 357 666 237 666Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-4-NCM-I-1D44B\" d=\"M834 683C817 683 758 680 741 680C721 680 651 683 631 683C616 683 608 675 608 659C608 650 613 645 624 644C648 641 660 633 660 618C660 609 654 598 642 585L486 417C457 485 429 552 400 620C401 622 402 624 405 626C414 636 431 642 455 644C471 646 479 654 479 667C479 678 473 683 460 683C436 683 359 680 335 680C314 680 245 683 224 683C209 683 202 675 202 659C202 649 211 644 229 644C274 644 288 644 300 615L416 342L208 119C205 116 201 112 196 109C155 66 108 42 53 39C36 38 27 29 27 14C30 5 31 0 44 0C61 0 120 3 137 3C158 3 227 0 248 0C263 0 270 8 270 24C270 33 265 38 254 39C230 41 218 50 218 65C218 75 227 89 244 107C306 173 369 239 430 306C464 225 499 145 532 63C531 61 530 59 527 56C518 47 502 42 478 39C463 37 455 29 455 16C455 5 461 0 474 0L599 3C620 3 690 0 708 0C723 0 731 8 731 23C731 33 724 38 709 39C678 39 658 40 651 44C644 48 636 60 628 80L501 383C569 455 620 509 653 546C677 571 693 588 702 595C739 626 780 642 825 644C846 645 851 650 851 669C848 678 847 683 834 683Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"msup\"><g data-mml-node=\"mn\"><use data-c=\"32\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-4-NCM-N-32\"></use></g><g data-mml-node=\"TeXAtom\" transform=\"translate(533,363) scale(0.707)\" data-mjx-texclass=\"ORD\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D44B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-4-NCM-I-1D44B\"></use></g></g></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container></span> to a carefully chosen collection of sets.</p><p>That collection must still support the standard operations consistens with measure properties: complements, countable unions, and therefore countable intersections.\n<span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight math-fallback\" data-math-prerendered=\"\" data-tex=\"\\sigma\" data-math-display=\"inline\" data-math-speech=\"sigma\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"Math: sigma. Original TeX: \\sigma\"><mjx-container class=\"MathJax math-svg\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.025ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.292ex\" height=\"1ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -431 571 442\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-5-NCM-I-1D70E\" d=\"M525 431L300 431C252 431 207 416 166 385C102 336 38 250 38 148C38 58 100-11 190-11C257-11 317 18 368 76C417 132 441 194 441 263C441 304 432 338 414 366L515 366C550 366 567 378 567 403C567 426 550 431 525 431M107 121C107 180 121 234 150 282C184 338 228 366 283 366C341 366 370 336 370 275C370 250 365 221 355 187C335 116 302 66 256 39C233 25 211 18 191 18C136 18 107 63 107 121Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D70E\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-5-NCM-I-1D70E\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container></span>-algebras supply exactly this structure.\nOnce the domain is in place, countable additivity yields the basic inequalities and continuity properties that make measures useful in later integration theory.</p>","a[href=\"#daily-lecture-log\"]":"<h2 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Daily lecture log<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#daily-lecture-log\" title=\"Link to this heading\">#</a></h2><p><span class=\"lecture-log-tentative\">Tentative</span>: Future lecture topics and their placement may change as the course develops.\n</p>","a[href=\"#calendar\"]":"<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">Calendar<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#calendar\" title=\"Link to this heading\">#</a></h1><p>I will put all deadlines on your <a class=\"reference external\" href=\"https://learn.uark.edu/ultra/courses/_529398_1/calendar/\">Blackboard Calendar</a>. Please check it regularly!</p>","a[href=\"lecture-notes/unit-1-introduction-to-measures/03-outer-measures.html\"]":"<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">3. Outer Measures<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#outer-measures\" title=\"Link to this heading\">#</a></h1><p>Our goal is to assign a meaningful notion of “size” to subsets of a space <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight math-fallback\" data-math-prerendered=\"\" data-tex=\"X\" data-math-display=\"inline\" data-math-speech=\"X\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"Math: X. Original TeX: X\"><mjx-container class=\"MathJax math-svg\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: 0;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"1.925ex\" height=\"1.545ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -683 851 683\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-1-NCM-I-1D44B\" d=\"M834 683C817 683 758 680 741 680C721 680 651 683 631 683C616 683 608 675 608 659C608 650 613 645 624 644C648 641 660 633 660 618C660 609 654 598 642 585L486 417C457 485 429 552 400 620C401 622 402 624 405 626C414 636 431 642 455 644C471 646 479 654 479 667C479 678 473 683 460 683C436 683 359 680 335 680C314 680 245 683 224 683C209 683 202 675 202 659C202 649 211 644 229 644C274 644 288 644 300 615L416 342L208 119C205 116 201 112 196 109C155 66 108 42 53 39C36 38 27 29 27 14C30 5 31 0 44 0C61 0 120 3 137 3C158 3 227 0 248 0C263 0 270 8 270 24C270 33 265 38 254 39C230 41 218 50 218 65C218 75 227 89 244 107C306 173 369 239 430 306C464 225 499 145 532 63C531 61 530 59 527 56C518 47 502 42 478 39C463 37 455 29 455 16C455 5 461 0 474 0L599 3C620 3 690 0 708 0C723 0 731 8 731 23C731 33 724 38 709 39C678 39 658 40 651 44C644 48 636 60 628 80L501 383C569 455 620 509 653 546C677 571 693 588 702 595C739 626 780 642 825 644C846 645 851 650 851 669C848 678 847 683 834 683Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mi\"><use data-c=\"1D44B\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-1-NCM-I-1D44B\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container></span>. The</p><p>An additive length cannot be defined on every subset of <span class=\"math notranslate nohighlight math-fallback\" data-math-prerendered=\"\" data-tex=\"\\R\" data-math-display=\"inline\" data-math-speech=\"backslash cap R\" role=\"math\" aria-label=\"Math: backslash cap R. Original TeX: \\R\"><mjx-container class=\"MathJax math-svg\" jax=\"SVG\" overflow=\"overflow\" aria-hidden=\"true\" focusable=\"false\"><svg style=\"vertical-align: -0.566ex;\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"2.796ex\" height=\"2.262ex\" role=\"img\" focusable=\"false\" viewBox=\"0 -750 1236 1000\" xmlns:xlink=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink\"><defs><path id=\"MJX-2-NCM-N-5C\" d=\"M420-250C436-250 444-242 444-226C444-223 444-220 443-218L101 734C97 745 90 750 79 750C63 750 55 742 55 726C55 723 55 720 56 718L398-234C402-245 409-250 420-250Z\"></path><path id=\"MJX-2-NCM-N-52\" d=\"M611 501C611 558 580 604 519 639C468 668 411 683 349 683L35 683L35 644L63 644C98 644 119 641 126 636C133 631 137 620 137 602L137 81C137 63 133 51 126 46C119 41 98 38 63 38L35 38L35-1C62 2 111 3 183 3C256 3 305 2 332-1L332 38L304 38C269 38 248 41 241 46C234 51 230 63 230 81L230 327L340 327C404 327 451 289 462 235C464 224 465 200 465 165C465 138 466 118 467 106C473 22 546-22 634-22C672-22 699-6 714 25C726 48 732 70 732 91C732 104 726 111 715 111C706 111 700 105 699 92C694 36 673 8 638 8C603 8 591 41 585 70C579 104 575 130 572 147L559 226C544 278 507 316 448 339C524 361 611 415 611 501M470 600C491 581 502 548 502 501C502 463 492 431 473 404C450 373 405 357 336 357L230 357L230 609C230 630 236 641 248 642C255 643 274 644 306 644C385 644 426 641 470 600Z\"></path></defs><g stroke=\"currentColor\" fill=\"currentColor\" stroke-width=\"0\" transform=\"scale(1,-1)\"><g data-mml-node=\"math\"><g data-mml-node=\"mtext\" fill=\"red\" stroke=\"red\"><use data-c=\"5C\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-2-NCM-N-5C\"></use><use data-c=\"52\" xlink:href=\"#MJX-2-NCM-N-52\" transform=\"translate(500,0)\"></use></g></g></g></svg></mjx-container></span>; a counterexample\ndue to Vitali, later in these notes will make the obstruction precise.\nAn <em>outer\nmeasure</em> is the useful first substitute. It is defined on all subsets, is\nbuilt from countable coverings, and is countably subadditive: the size of a\nunion is no greater than the sum of the sizes used to cover it.</p>","a[href=\"lecture-notes/unit-1-introduction-to-measures/01-review-of-riemann-integration.html\"]":"<h1 class=\"tippy-header\" style=\"margin-top: 0;\">1. Review of Riemann Integration<a class=\"headerlink\" href=\"#review-of-riemann-integration\" title=\"Link to this heading\">#</a></h1><p>We briefly review the construction and properties of the Riemann integral and identify its shortcomings that suggest constructing a new integration theory: the Lebesgue integral.</p><p>First, we show that continuous functions are a natural class on which the\nRiemann integral is defined. Some discontinuities can also be tolerated. The\nexact criterion, proved later in the course, is expressed through the\nmeasure-theoretic size of the discontinuity set.</p>"}
skip_classes = ["headerlink", "sd-stretched-link", "no-hover-preview"]

window.onload = function () {
    for (const [select, tip_html] of Object.entries(selector_to_html)) {
        const links = document.querySelectorAll(`article.bd-article ${select}`);
        for (const link of links) {
            if (skip_classes.some(c => link.classList.contains(c))) {
                continue;
            }

            tippy(link, {
                content: tip_html,
                allowHTML: true,
                arrow: true,
                placement: 'auto-start', maxWidth: 500, interactive: false,
                onShow(instance) {MathJax.typesetPromise([instance.popper]).then(() => {});},
            });
        };
    };
    console.log("tippy tips loaded!");
};
